{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 28, :comments ["<b>And Haran died in the lifetime of Terah his father, in the land of his birth, in Ur of the Chaldees.</b> This verse informs us of the merit of Abraham, that he surrendered himself to death for the faith in YHWH, blessed be He. And had it not been for the hint in this verse, it would have been fitting to inform us first of the merit of Abraham and his conduct and his qualities, and only then, 'and YHWH said to Abram' — for how is it conceivable that there be a divine word to a person without first informing us who he is? After all, the word did not come to Noah, 'and Elohim said to Noah,' until He first informed us of his qualities — that he was 'a righteous man, wholehearted.' Therefore the hint came in this verse, bearing testimony to the merit of Abraham in the matter of Ur of the Chaldees, on account of which it was fitting that the Shekhinah speak with him. This is because Abraham's native land was not Ur of the Chaldees but rather across the River, as it says, 'across the River your fathers dwelt of old' (Joshua 24:2), and the word מעולם ('of old') testifies to this. From this the verse says (Genesis 12): ״ויגד לאברם העברי״ ('And they told Abram the Hebrew'), it did not say 'Abram the Chaldean.' And a proof for the matter: Nahor was in Haran, and if his place had been Ur of the Chaldees in the land of Shinar, and the verse relates that when they departed from Ur of the Chaldees he took with him only Abram his son and Lot his grandson and Sarai his daughter-in-law, then Nahor would have remained in the land of the Chaldees. But in truth his native land was the land of Aram, across the River; and so we find in the words of our Rabbis of blessed memory in the Talmud, that Abraham was imprisoned in Kutha, and Kutha is not in the land of the Chaldees but across the River." "<b>And know</b> that Terah fathered his elder sons Abram and Nahor across the River, in the land of his fathers, and went with his son Abram to the land of the Chaldees, and there his youngest son Haran was born, while his son Nahor remained across the River in that city. And this is the sense of ״בארץ מולדתו באור כשדים״ ('in the land of his birth, in Ur of the Chaldees'), for the word מולדתו ('his birth') refers back to Haran, for it is the land of birth of Haran alone." "<b>And the Rambam</b> of blessed memory wrote in the Guide for the Perplexed that it is mentioned in the book of the worship of the Egyptian farmers that Abram was born in Kutha, and he disputed the opinion of the masses who were sun-worshippers, and the king placed him in prison, and he remained there with them in reproof for many days. Afterward the king feared lest he corrupt his land for him and lest he turn the creatures away from their faith, and he banished him to the edge of the land of Canaan after taking all his wealth. End quote. And as for his using the term אור כשדים, know that the word אור embraces three meanings: a valley, a mountain, and fire. Valley — in the manner of (Isaiah 11:8) ״מאורת צפעוני״ ('the hole of the adder'), for the verse calls the hole of an adder מאורה, and the בקעה is the valley, so it is as though it said 'in the valley of the Chaldees.' Mountain — in the manner of ״באורים כבדו ה'״ ('on the heights, glorify YHWH'), for they called the high mountains 'urim' because they light fires there and raise signal-flares there from mountain to mountain, to proclaim the new months across great distances in a single day; and this is what it says (Isaiah 24:15) ״באיי הים שם ה' אלהי ישראל״ — that they would proclaim throughout the world the miracle and the wonder done for them to the glory of the Omnipresent, blessed be He; and it is as though it said 'the mountain of the Chaldees.' Fire — in the manner of (Isaiah 44:16) ״חמותי ראיתי אור״ ('I am warm, I have seen fire'); and this is the opinion of our Rabbis of blessed memory and the true received tradition, that they cast him into the fiery furnace. Now in any case the miracle for Abraham our father was performed in the land of the Chaldees — either a hidden miracle, that He placed it in the king's heart not to kill him and to bring him out of prison, from that valley or from that mountain; or a publicized miracle, that they cast him into the fiery furnace and he was delivered."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:11:54.180742791Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}